<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mannheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mannheim is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a>. Founded as a fortress town by Elector Frederick IV of the Palatinate in 1606 and laid out in a grid of lettered blocks, it became the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> Palatine in 1720. Under Charles Theodore its court orchestra made it a musical centre. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1803 and became an industrial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mannheim.jpg" alt="Mannheim" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Buzin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altes_Rathaus_und_Sankt_Sebastianskirche_auf_dem_Mannheimer_Marktplatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
